[ti:PISA 2022: Students' Historic Setbacks]
[by:VOA Learning English]

[00:01.54]Students around the world suffered historic setbacks in reading and math during the COVID-19 pandemic.
[00:12.04]That information comes from results of the latest Program for International Student Assessment, or PISA.
[00:22.45]The PISA is an international comparative study of the scores of 15-year-old students on tests in reading, math and science.
[00:34.51]Nearly 700,000 students took part in the study.
[00:40.86]PISA is administered every three years.
[00:44.92]The latest results showed an unprecedented drop in performance since 2018.
[00:53.31]The PISA study was supposed to happen in 2021, but it was postponed because of the pandemic.
[01:04.57]The 2022 results were released on December 5th.
[01:10.12]It is the first extensive study with data on how the pandemic has affected student performance around the world.
[01:21.45]The study looked at 81 education systems.
[01:26.41]They include 37 member countries of the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development, or OECD.
[01:38.48]OECD has been administering the test since 2000.
[01:46.31]The study found decreasing test scores in both rich and poor countries.
[01:53.84]Andreas Schleicher is the director for education and skills at OECD.
[02:01.82]He said the world is no longer divided between rich and well-educated countries and poor and badly educated countries.
[02:13.41]The study found the average international math score fell by 15 points since 2018.
[02:22.61]Reading scores across participating countries fell by 10 points.
[02:29.11]The OECD considers 20 points to be equal to one year of learning.
[02:36.99]Scores did not change in a major way in science.
[02:43.34]Albania saw the biggest decrease in math scores with a massive 69-point decrease.
[02:51.66]That means Albanian students lost more than three years of learning.
[02:58.48]Many countries fell by more than one year's worth of learning in math, including Germany, Iceland and the Netherlands.
[03:09.46]Four places did improve their performance between PISA 2018 and PISA 2022 in all three subjects.
[03:19.73]Brunei, Cambodia, the Dominican Republic and Chinese Taipei.
[03:26.15]Six East Asian education systems outperformed all other countries in math.
[03:33.82]Scores in Singapore were measurably higher than all other countries in all three subjects.
[03:42.69]The math scores for U.S. students were about even with the international average.
[03:50.30]Science and reading were slightly higher.
[03:55.01]The COVID-19 pandemic was a major reason for the worldwide score drops, but science and reading scores were dropping even before the pandemic.
[04:07.78]This suggests there are other reasons for the decrease.
[04:14.21]Factors such as the level of investment in education, the social value and pay levels of teachers, and educational beliefs of students have all influenced educational results.
[04:30.10]The OECD's Schleicher said.
[04:34.68]The PISA tests explore how well students can solve complex problems, think critically and communicate effectively.
[04:45.97]The goal of PISA is to provide useful information to educators and policymakers on the strengths and weaknesses of their country's education system.
[04:59.86]OECD Secretary-General Mathias Cormann released the latest PISA results in Paris.
[05:09.22]He said PISA helps to identify the comparative strengths of education systems that have performed well despite recent shocks.
[05:22.68]Policymakers can use such information to consider reforms to education systems for a brighter, more financially successful future, Cormann added.
[05:36.47]The study also reported on the fast-changing influence of technology on educational performance.
[05:45.47]The results found that moderate use of electronic devices in school is connected to higher performance.
